WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:06.160
- [Narrator] A coronary
artery bypass graft, or CABG,

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:09.370
is performed to improve
circulation to the heart muscle

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:12.393
in people with severe
coronary artery disease.

00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:16.840
In this procedure, a
healthy artery or vein

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:19.730
from another part of the body is connected

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:22.623
or grafted to the blocked coronary artery.

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:27.570
The grafted artery or vein
bypasses the blocked portion

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:29.390
of the coronary artery,

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:32.093
carrying oxygen-rich
blood to the heart muscle.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:36.400
One or more coronary
arteries may be bypassed

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:38.083
during a single operation.

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:42.890
Before the surgery, an
intravenous line will be started,

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:45.713
and you may be given a
medication to help you relax.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:50.310
CABG procedures are done
under general anesthesia,

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:51.460
which will put you to sleep

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:54.290
for the duration of the operation.

00:00:54.290 --> 00:00:56.990
A breathing tube will be
inserted through your mouth

00:00:56.990 --> 00:00:59.293
and into your throat to help you breathe.

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:01.690
A catheter will be placed

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:03.993
in your bladder to drain your urine.

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:07.330
Coronary artery bypass surgery

00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:10.283
generally takes three to six hours.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.730
During a conventional bypass surgery,

00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:16.180
your surgeon begins by making an incision

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:20.050
in the skin over your
breastbone or sternum.

00:01:20.050 --> 00:01:23.660
He or she will then cut the
sternum and move your ribcage

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:25.313
in order to get to your heart.

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:27.410
Throughout the procedure,

00:01:27.410 --> 00:01:29.880
your circulatory system will be connected

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:33.773
to a cardiopulmonary bypass
pump or heart-lung machine.

00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:37.570
This machine temporarily
performs the functions

00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:40.320
of your heart and lungs
during the surgery,

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:42.050
allowing your heart to be stopped

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:44.873
while the surgeon sews
the grafts into place.

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:49.720
One of two blood vessels is
typically used for the grafts:

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:53.120
the internal thoracic artery in the chest

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:55.023
or the saphenous vein in the leg.

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:58.700
For the internal thoracic artery graft,

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:01.460
your surgeon will leave
the upper end attached

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:04.840
to the subclavian artery
and divert the lower end

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:07.760
from your chest wall
to your coronary artery

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:09.333
just beyond the blockage.

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:14.303
Your surgeon will then
sow the graft into place.

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:17.790
For the saphenous vein graft,

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:20.920
your surgeon will suture
one end to the aorta

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:23.420
and the other end to the narrowed artery

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:25.730
just beyond the blockage.

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:28.090
With the graft securely in place,

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:30.580
your surgeon will use
use electrical signals

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:32.210
to restore the heartbeat

00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:34.853
and attach a temporary
pacemaker to the heart.

00:02:36.130 --> 00:02:38.560
Once your heart is again beating normally,

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:40.763
the heart-lung machine
will be disconnected.

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:46.320
Your surgeon will wire the
breastbone back together

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:49.220
and suture the skin incision closed.

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:51.980
A temporary drainage tube will be placed

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:54.463
through the skin beneath the incision.

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:57.750
Two other types of CABG procedures

00:02:57.750 --> 00:02:59.830
have recently been developed:

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:03.423
off-pump and minimally
invasive bypass surgery.

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:11.300
Off-pump CABG is also called
beating heart bypass grafting

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:13.330
because the heart isn't stopped

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:16.390
and a heart-lung machine isn't used.

00:03:16.390 --> 00:03:19.960
Instead, a mechanical device
is used to steady the part

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:22.433
of the heart where grafting is being done.

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:27.310
Surgeons perform minimally
invasive bypass procedures

00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:29.810
using specially designed instruments

00:03:29.810 --> 00:03:34.610
inserted through small
incisions or ports in the chest.

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:38.800
These procedures sometimes
require a heart-lung machine.

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:42.580
After surgery, you will be taken
to the intensive care unit.

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:45.830
The activity of your heart
will be carefully monitored.

00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:48.680
If necessary, the pacing
wires will be used

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:51.023
to temporarily control your heart rate.

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:54.100
The chest tube will remain in place

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:57.233
to drain excess blood and
air from the chest cavity.

00:03:58.070 --> 00:03:59.810
Once you can breathe on your own,

00:03:59.810 --> 00:04:01.740
your breathing tube will be removed

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:04.630
and replaced with an oxygen mask.

00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:07.730
The bladder catheter will remain in place.

00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:10.770
As you recover over the
next three to four days,

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:14.253
all of these devices will
be gradually removed.

